  "response": "Adjudicating between the two explanations is a design problem rather than an interpretive one. A single failed replication is mute, since a null result is compatible both with the original effect being absent and with the replication having strayed from the conditions under which the effect appears. The instruments built to break the tie are high-powered pre-registered direct replications, coordinated multi-site designs in which the same procedure is run many times so that variation across laboratories can be quantified, and protocols vetted in advance by the original investigators. In 2015 the Open Science Collaboration published replications of 100 psychology studies, and as a result ninety-seven percent of the original studies in the Reproducibility Project: Psychology had reported statistically significant results. The mean effect size in the Reproducibility Project: Psychology replications was roughly half the mean effect size of the original studies.\n\nOf those replications, 36 produced statistically significant effects in the original direction, which shows that fewer than half of the Reproducibility Project: Psychology replications reached statistical significance. Either that minority tally stands, or at least half of the Reproducibility Project: Psychology replications reached statistical significance; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be. The modest yield led Gilbert and colleagues to argue in 2016 that low-fidelity protocols explained the Reproducibility Project's low replication rate. Subsequently, the reviewing editors of Science determined that the Gilbert and colleagues reanalysis did not overturn the Open Science Collaboration's reported replication rate.\n\nMany Labs 2 tested 28 published findings in more than 60 laboratories around the world, and that broad sampling frame resulted in an estimate of heterogeneity across samples which showed heterogeneity to be modest for most of the effects tested. Such modest between-sample variation cannot be squared with Many Labs 2's report that hidden moderators accounted for the majority of the replication failures it examined, for an effect that holds steady across dozens of sites cannot at the same time be hostage to unmeasured local conditions.\n\nAfter Many Labs 2 had finished its multi-site survey, the hidden-moderator defense was put to a still more direct test, one in which the architects of the original studies were allowed to specify the conditions themselves. Many Labs 5 revised replication protocols with input from the original authors and from peer reviewers, and that revision yielded effect sizes no larger than those obtained from the earlier replication protocols. The parity of those effect sizes cannot be squared with Many Labs 5's demonstration that expert-revised protocols raised replication success to the rate observed in the original publications, since a revision cannot both leave the measured effects exactly where they stood and lift success to the level of the original reports.\n\nWhat this decade of work establishes, then, is a procedure rather than a verdict about psychology as a whole. Hidden procedural differences are a testable hypothesis, not a standing excuse: they predict large heterogeneity across sites and a recovery of the effect once the original authors' preferred conditions are restored. Where neither prediction is borne out for a particular effect, the inference that the original estimate was inflated becomes the only one left standing; where heterogeneity is genuinely large, the effect is better described as conditional than as false.",
